== KV5-7c-i: retention behind the base and the deferred release (the window pool still full-size)
TestKVWindow_retentionIsBitIdentical on the 11 fixtures: 1,536 positions prefilled in blocks of 256 and 64 decoded on scrambled tables, every logit equal to retention off; the window group holds from 1,088 of 1,540 positions, 17 blocks released, the pool's counts equal to its tables. The rewind (100 positions), the write below the base (refused; ReusableKV answers 0), CopyKV from a released sequence and the reset (base 0, nothing retired) hold. Plants red on all 11: one that keeps nothing behind the block being written and clears each freed block, one that frees a block and leaves its entry naming it. TestKVWindow_retiredBlocksWaitForTheirBuffers (with 50 of 100 buffers complete every retired entry still names its block; with 100, none is pending; the early-free plant red) and TestKVWindow_reusableKVIsTheWindowOracle (the base read one block low red) pass.
A one-block-early release cannot show on logits behind kvWindowKeep's 448 retained positions, so the registered plant releases up to the end of the write. A freed block is not cleared, so a plant that frees and re-takes one block in a token left the rows intact on five fixtures until it also cleared the block (the plant was weak, not retention).
Metal package whole 493 PASS / 238 SKIP / 0 FAIL (13:02:08-13:10:00); identity 30 / 0 / 14; no priced byte and no census group moved.

== KV5-7c-ii: a bounded window pool, sized and priced by one function the Metal backend registers
Pool: decoder.MetalKVWindowBlocks(window) = ceil((window - 1 + max(448, 2,048)) / 64) + 4 blocks a slot. The conditions that keep it the context's size (backend/metal/kv_window_pool.go): f32 KV, MoE, MLA, recurrent state, attention sinks, learned positions, LayerNorm, per-layer geometry, E-models, a drafter's KV, a prefill feature or step the batched pass lacks, the q4k lane, exact prefill or the fast-prefill knobs off, an unaligned width, a head dim over 256; models whose layers all slide keep the context's pool.
TestKVWindow_planAndBuildAgree: the Metal backend registered the function; 11 fixtures have a window group beside a full one and 4 are bounded by the conditions (gemma2, gemma2-hd, gemma3-vl, olmo3); on those, the plan and the build agree, and for each of three variants (f32 KV, the fast-prefill knob off, exact prefill) the condition keeps the pool full-size and the plant that ignores it makes the plan bound a pool whose build prefill is unsplit (red on all 4, each variant).
The forced-bound arms of TestKVWindow_retentionIsBitIdentical (the pool bounded on all 11, whatever the conditions): decoded logits equal the context's pool with every position kept, the audit clean, and the same after CopyKV.
gemma3-1b (an int4 .giw, f16 KV, window 512), TestKVWindow_gemma3PricingTable, ResidentKVBytes on Metal for one slot, every position held against bounded (44 blocks a slot):
  ctx  4,096    105.6 MiB   78.1 MiB  1.35x
  ctx 16,384    417.6 MiB  126.1 MiB  3.31x
  ctx 32,768    833.6 MiB  190.1 MiB  4.38x
gemma-3-4b-it (Q4_K_M, int4): not loaded, the fit guard refused it (about 6.9 GB needed, 6.7 GB available with the owner's apps open); listed, not bypassed, and not read.
gemma3-1b through the decoder's own route, TestKVWindow_gemma3RealIdentity, a 6,000-token prompt and 96 greedy tokens, then a 6,101-token second turn that reuses the first turn's KV and 64 more: the same 96 + 64 tokens with the 44-block bounded pool and the 128-block context pool. The first run of this test ended the turn at once with the bounded pool (budget 0): retention ran after a write had taken its blocks, so a 3,000-token prompt in two passes held the old window and the new pass together and overflowed 44 blocks. kvWrite now retires what its own reads leave behind before it takes blocks.
Metal package whole 494 PASS / 240 SKIP / 0 FAIL, exit 0 (13:57:43-14:06:07; the two new skips are the heavy-gated readings above, run separately); identity against the 7c-i tip 30 IDENTICAL / 0 DIFFERENT / 14 NOT RUN; touched.py, the lint and the parity refresh (forward goldens 43 passed, 0 failed, 21 skipped; 0 deps_hash lines) pass.
Census: none of the three classes moves for 7c-ii. The decision census runs in package decoder, which does not link the Metal backend, so the registered function is absent there and the plan prices the window pool full-size; TestDecisionCensus passes unchanged on the Mac.
A binary without the Metal backend, and the host fit guards before any model exists, price Metal's window pool full-size: more than a bounded build allocates, the safe direction.

